[2. Step 2: The Response DTO]
We should not return a raw string. A professional API returns a JSON object. Create a class AuthResponse.java.
package org.adesh.security1.Dto;

public class AuthResponse {
    private String token;

    public AuthResponse(String token) {
        this.token = token;
    }

    public String getToken() {
        return token;
    }
}

[3. Step 3: Update AuthController]
Now we combine everything: AuthenticationManager (Verify) + JwtService (Generate).
package org.adesh.security1.Controller;

import org.adesh.security1.Dto.AuthResponse;
import org.adesh.security1.Dto.LoginRequest;
import org.adesh.security1.Entity.User;
import org.adesh.security1.Repository.UserRepository;
import org.adesh.security1.Service.JwtService; // Your Service
import org.springframework.beans.factory.annotation.Autowired;
import org.springframework.http.ResponseEntity;
import org.springframework.security.authentication.AuthenticationManager;
import org.springframework.security.authentication.UsernamePasswordAuthenticationToken;
import org.springframework.security.core.Authentication;
import org.springframework.security.core.userdetails.UsernameNotFoundException;
import org.springframework.security.crypto.password.PasswordEncoder;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.*;

@RestController
@RequestMapping("/api/auth")
public class AuthController {

    @Autowired
    private AuthenticationManager authenticationManager; // 1. The Boss (Verify)

    @Autowired
    private JwtService jwtService; // 2. The Machine (Generate Token)

    @Autowired
    private UserRepository userRepository;

    @Autowired
    private PasswordEncoder passwordEncoder;

    // ==========================================
    // 1. REGISTER (Standard - Create User)
    // ==========================================
    @PostMapping("/register")
    public ResponseEntity<String> register(@RequestBody LoginRequest request) {
        if (userRepository.findByUsername(request.getUsername()).isPresent()) {
            return ResponseEntity.badRequest().body("Username taken!");
        }

        User user = new User();
        user.setUsername(request.getUsername());
        user.setPassword(passwordEncoder.encode(request.getPassword())); // Hash it!
        user.setRole("ROLE_USER");
        
        userRepository.save(user);

        return ResponseEntity.ok("User registered successfully!");
    }

    // ==========================================
    // 2. LOGIN (The Full Flow)
    // ==========================================
    @PostMapping("/login")
    public ResponseEntity<AuthResponse> login(@RequestBody LoginRequest request) {
        
        // Step A: VERIFY Credentials (The Black Box)
        // If this fails, it throws exception and stops here.
        Authentication authentication = authenticationManager.authenticate(
            new UsernamePasswordAuthenticationToken(
                request.getUsername(), 
                request.getPassword()
            )
        );

        // Step B: RETRIEVE User
        // If we reached here, password is correct. Get the user data.
        // We query the DB again to get the full object for the Token generation.
        User user = userRepository.findByUsername(request.getUsername())
                .orElseThrow(() -> new UsernameNotFoundException("User not found"));

        // Step C: GENERATE Token
        String token = jwtService.generateJwtToken(user);

        // Step D: RETURN Token
        return ResponseEntity.ok(new AuthResponse(token));
    }
}

[4. Line-by-Line Code Explanation]
Focus on /login:
 * authenticationManager.authenticate(...):
   * Input: "adesh" / "12345"
   * Action: Checks DB hash.
   * Result: Passes (Go to next line) or Crashes (Return 403).
 * userRepository.findByUsername(...):
   * Reason: The JwtService needs a User object to extract the id and role to put inside the token.
 * jwtService.generateJwtToken(user):
   * Action: Takes the User ID (5), Name (adesh), Role (USER).
   * Action: Signs it with your Secret Key.
   * Result: Produces string eyJhbGci....
 * return ResponseEntity.ok(new AuthResponse(token)):
   * Result: JSON {"token": "eyJhbGci..."}.
